.TH CTPOOL_SHUTDOWN_IMMEDIATE 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_shutdown_immediate \- immediate shutdown
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_shutdown_immediate(ctpool pool);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Cancels all tasks currently in the queue, then signals worker threads
to exit after their current task (if any) completes. Blocks until all
worker threads have exited.

Futures for cancelled tasks are marked done and cancelled; any thread
blocked in ctpool_future_get for a cancelled future is unblocked and
receives NULL. Calling ctpool_shutdown_immediate a second time is a
no-op.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
pool   Thread pool handle (CTPOOL_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle is a no-op)
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Tasks that are already executing when this is called are allowed to
finish normally.
ctpool_submit and ctpool_submit_future return ccol_not_permitted after
this call.

Calling ctpool_shutdown_immediate on pool from within a task (or that
task's on_complete callback) currently executing on one of pool's own
worker threads is a complete no-op, for the same reason as the
identical case documented for ctpool_shutdown_drain: a worker thread
cannot join itself.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_shutdown_drain (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_destroy (3)
